  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🪞 Brow Business Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a brow bar, brow studio, brow tech or threading business."
    },
    "intro": "Good brow business names are short, neat and a little playful, and many use the shape of the brow itself: <em>Soft Arch Threading Studio</em>, <em>Perfect Frame Brows</em>, <em>Brows Up</em>. The **Brow Business Name Generator** suggests names for a brow bar, eyebrow studio, brow tech, lamination or threading business, and brow and lash studios: catchy, cute, modern, luxury, aesthetic, your town or your own name.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good brow business names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Brows Up</strong>: short, upbeat and easy to fit on a sign.</li><li><strong>Tint & Tweeze</strong>: two treatments in three words, with a bit of alliteration.</li><li><strong>Perfect Frame Brows</strong>: brows frame the face, so the name says why they matter.</li><li><strong>Les Beaux Sourcils</strong>: French for \"the beautiful eyebrows\", for a luxury studio.</li><li><strong>Taupe Threading Lounge</strong>: taupe is a classic brow-pencil shade.</li><li><strong>Little Hummingbird Brow & Lash Bar</strong>: cute, light and clear about both services.</li><li><strong>Juniper & Fig Brow Bar</strong>: an aesthetic, Instagram-friendly pairing.</li><li><strong>Henna Brow & Threading Studio</strong>: names the treatments clients search for.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Brows, lashes or threading?",
        "content": "<p>Choose what you offer, and the names follow:</p><ul><li><strong>Brows</strong> covers shaping, waxing, tinting, lamination and henna brows. Words like arch, frame, shape and lift work here.</li><li><strong>Brows and lashes</strong> adds \"Brow &amp; Lash\" to the name, so clients see both at a glance.</li><li><strong>Threading</strong> uses a twisted cotton thread to remove hair in lines. It's a traditional technique in South Asia and the Middle East, and many threading bars name themselves for speed and precision.</li></ul><p>The ending matters too. A <em>Bar</em> sounds quick and walk-in; a <em>Studio</em>, <em>Room</em> or <em>Atelier</em> sounds like an appointment for something longer, such as lamination.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>A name only works if nobody else nearby has it. Before you order the sign, the cards or the van wrap, press <strong>Check</strong> on any name, or use the <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a>:</p><ul><li><strong>Domain and handles.</strong> Most clients will find you on Instagram or Google first, so get the .co.uk or .com and the same handle everywhere if you can.</li><li><strong>The company register.</strong> In the UK, a limited company name can't be the same as one already on Companies House. In the US, search your state's business register.</li><li><strong>Trade marks.</strong> Search the UK IPO or USPTO register for anything in your field, as a registered mark can stop you using a name even if the company register allowed it.</li><li><strong>Your area.</strong> Search Google Maps for brow bars and threading near you. A similar name a few streets away will confuse clients and reviews.</li></ul><p>This is general guidance, not legal advice. For the full process, read <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Making it yours",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Keep it short for Instagram,</strong> where most brow clients find you. Your handle should match the name.</li><li><strong>Avoid pinning yourself to one trend.</strong> \"Lami\" and \"henna\" names are great now; if trends change, a general name lasts longer.</li><li><strong>Add your town</strong> if you rely on walk-ins: <em>Hampton Threading Bar</em> is exactly what people search for.</li></ul><p>For the rest of the face, try the <a href=\"lash-business-name-generator.html\">Lash Business</a>, <a href=\"makeup-artist-name-generator.html\">Makeup Artist</a> and <a href=\"spray-tan-business-name-generator.html\">Spray Tan Business</a> name generators.</p><p>For more, see all the <a href=\"../categories/biz_beauty-names.html\">beauty and wellness business name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What are good brow business names?",
        "answer": "Short, neat names about the brow's shape or what you do, like Brows Up, Perfect Frame Brows or Tint & Tweeze. Choose Luxury for names like Les Beaux Sourcils, or Threading for a threading bar."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a brow bar, and should I call my business one?",
        "answer": "A brow bar is a small salon, or a counter inside a shop, that only does eyebrows: shaping, threading, waxing, tinting and lamination, usually quickly and without an appointment. \"Brow Bar\" suits a walk-in counter; \"Brow Studio\" or \"Brow Room\" suits appointments and longer treatments such as lamination or henna. If you also do lash lifts, \"Brow & Lash\" tells clients at a glance."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I check a brow business name is free?",
        "answer": "Press Check on any name to see whether it's free as a company, a domain and a social media handle. In the UK, also search Companies House; in the US, search your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search. Check your local area too, as another business nearby with a similar name can confuse clients."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does sourcils mean?",
        "answer": "Sourcils is French for eyebrows, so Les Beaux Sourcils means \"the beautiful eyebrows\". French words suit a luxury brow studio, but check the spelling and say it out loud before you commit."
      }
    ]
  },
